1) Class+Responsibilities
Shell: Acts as the central user interface manager. It runs a loop that captures keyboard input using,
parses text into tokens, and safely routes execution paths to decouple command from algorithmic logic.

AsciiArtAlgorithm: does a single operational run mapping images into character arrays. takes specific
instances of image data and matching components to generate grid, serving as a high level controller.

feature: A strategy pattern interface defining a contract via execute(String args).
This design choice leverages polymorphism, enabling the shell to dynamically execute
user commands without knowing their specific underlying logic.

add: Implements the feature interface to parse and handle token arguments for adding characters.
This isolates the parsing grammar of character entry into its own dedicated class.

remove: Implements the feature interface to safely extract characters from the active set.
encapsulating the removal grammar to ensure the shell stays lean and clean.

reverse: Implements the feature interface to change the reverseState. Interacts with character matcher
to invert how symbols match image pixels, encapsulating the trigger logic away from the shell loop.

Output: Implements the feature interface and manages the current output type.
Uses the AsciiOutput interface to switch between console output and HTML output
without changing the shell code.

Res: Implements the feature interface and manages the current image resolution.
Checks resolution limits according to the image size and updates the number
of characters per row based on user commands.
This keeps the resolution logic separate from the shell code.

Chars: Implements the feature interface and prints the current character set
in sorted ASCII order. This keeps the printing logic separate from the shell code.

ImageProcessor: Responsible for padding images, splitting them into sub-images, and calculating brightness values for
ASCII matching.
Also stores the last brightness matrix to avoid repeated calculations
between runs with the same resolution.

AsciiExceptions: A checked exception class handling validation, syntax, and execution errors.
separates error string assembly from active program logic, using the Single Responsibility Principle.

SubImgCharMatcher: Matches subimage brightness levels to the closest ASCII symbol. coordinates the active
character, performs linear stretch stretching, and isolates its matching data from the outer shell.

BrightnessAscii: Computes and stores the unnormalized raw brightness values for characters.
maintaining Single Responsibility Principle by keeping character calculations out of other components.

CharConverter: Renders standard font characters directly into binary boolean matrix blocks. // we didnt changed
Image: Bundles  pixel layouts into clean matrix grids // we didnt changed


2) Data Structures for Efficiency
in BrightnessAscii: double[] AsciiNumArray
It optimizes memory usage by maintaining an array of exactly 95 slots (the legal ASCII characters)
and provides constant time O(1) lookup for any character's raw brightness.
we chose this because raw brightness doesn't change.

in SubImgCharMatcher:
HashSet<Character> characters: keeps track of unique active chars dynamically.
This choice guarantees fast O(1) average time complexity for additions, removals,
and verification checks when modifying the active pool.

TreeMap<Double, Character> NormBToCharTreeMap: Maps normalized brightness scales to characters.
This allows us to find floor and ceiling keys in efficient logarithmic O(log N) time,
optimizing matching speeds during the pixel to character loop

in Shell:
Map<String, feature> featureMap: Maps command strings to their feature instances.
This replaces nested conditional blocks with clean O(1) constant time lookups.

in Chars:
TreeSet<Character> charset:
We used TreeSet because it keeps characters sorted according to ASCII order, helps us print the active charset,
and also prevents duplicate characters while maintaining efficient
O(log N) insertion, removal, and lookup operations.

in ImageProcessor:
double[][] lastBrightnessArr:Stores the evaluated subimage matrix. saving calculations across consecutive
execution runs avoids redundant processing cycles, heavily optimizing the program's overall runtime efficiency.


3) Exception Handling Strategy:
*Single Responsibility Principle: We encapsulated all parsing,syntax, and boundary violations inside a class
called AsciiExceptions. This keeps our user interface classes free from string building or logging code.
*Static Factory Pattern: Instead of creating separate small files, we used descriptive static factory methods
within a single exception class. This lets us cleanly reuse common error messages and constants.
*Information Hiding: Reusable error strings are stored as private static constants.
*Checked Exceptions for Type Safety: Inheriting directly from the standard Java Exception class forces
explicit safety tracking through compile time throws declarations. This ensures compile time safety.

4) SubImgCharMatcher API Alterations:
public void changeReverse(): Added to let the shell change the reverse mapping state.
This enforces clear encapsulation and information hiding, as the shell doesn't need to know how
the mathematical inversion is calculated.

public TreeSet<Character> getCharset(): Added to safely expose a sorted snapshot of the active symbol collection.
Returning a new instance preserves defensive copying principles, preventing outer classes
from corrupting internal collections.

These changes keep character mapping calculations completely encapsulated inside the matcher component,
ensuring that how an image's brightness corresponds to a character is managed in one single place.

5) No additional changes were made to the APIs of the provided classes.
